<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Wild- and Rhinegraviate</h1><p class="st-meta">1409 CE – 1794 CE (approx.) · capital Kirn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A county on the Nahe and in the Hunsrück whose Wild- and Rhinegraves, ancestors of the princes of Salm, ruled from castles above Kirn.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Wildgraves held lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a>, while the Rhinegraves ruled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rheingrafenstein">Rheingrafenstein</a>. In 1409 the Rhinegrave John III inherited the Wildgraviate, uniting both titles as Wild- and Rhinegraves. A marriage in 1475 later brought them half of the county of Salm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The family repeatedly divided its lands into branches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaun-castle">Dhaun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrburg">Kyrburg</a> and Grumbach, with Kyrburg castle above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirn">Kirn</a> as a main seat. Most branches became Lutheran, though some Salm lines returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. The counts often served as officers in French and imperial armies, and branches were raised to princes as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salm">Salm-Salm</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salm-1802">Salm-Kyrburg</a> in the 17th and 18th centuries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> occupied the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1794, ending the family's rule there. Compensated in 1803 with secularised lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, the princes of Salm formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salm">Principality of Salm</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed in 1811.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1409 CE – 1794 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.74%;width:7.66%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1409 CE – 1794 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kirn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wild- und Rheingrafschaft, Salm-Kyrburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1602&amp;focus=wild-and-rhinegraviate" aria-label="Wild- and Rhinegraviate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4851 444 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wild- and Rhinegraviate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5171 700L5229 694M5171 700L5054 728M5171 700L5149 641"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0M5149 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5171 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5171 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kirn. <a href="/atlas?year=1602&amp;focus=wild-and-rhinegraviate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
